{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"Torres v. Madrid\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2021-03-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"592 U.S. 306\",\"proposition\":\"Physical force applied to a person with objective intent to restrain is a Fourth Amendment seizure even when the person escapes; the seizure lasts only for the force’s application. Recent circuit decisions have made the objective-intent, unintended-target, and indirect-contact boundaries newly consequential.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / Torres baseline\",\"discovery_path\":\"Recent physical-force-seizure commentary → 2024–2026 circuit applications and acknowledged split → controlling SCOTUS precedent\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"New York State Rifle & Pistol Ass’n v. Bruen\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2022-06-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"597 U.S. 1\",\"proposition\":\"Although a Second Amendment case, Bruen’s recognition of lawful public carry is now reshaping Terry analysis: firearm possession or a firearm-shaped bulge cannot automatically imply illegality or dangerousness where public carry is lawful.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry reasonable suspicion / lawful firearm possession\",\"discovery_path\":\"Post-Bruen Terry-stop commentary → Fifth Circuit’s 2025 Wilson decision and circuit-split materials\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Stewart\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-02-02\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"92 F.4th 461\",\"proposition\":\"Applied Rodriguez’s two-stage inquiry and held that cumulative observations—including evasive or inconsistent travel answers, a third-party vehicle, tint, criminal history, and corridor context—supplied reasonable suspicion before the traffic mission ended.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / Rodriguez extension / reasonable suspicion\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 precedential Rodriguez queries → Third Circuit opinion → cumulative-factor follow-up\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Coco v. Dear\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1787\",\"proposition\":\"Police use of a mounted horse to direct or crowd a protester did not itself seize her where she retained freedom to evade the horse; a physical-force seizure occurred when an officer intentionally grabbed her shirt.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / crowd control / objective restraint\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Torres protest-force query → Third Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ramirez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"98 F.4th 1141\",\"proposition\":\"Asking a stopped driver about parole status and conducting a criminal-history check were negligibly burdensome officer-safety precautions within the traffic-stop mission and therefore did not unlawfully prolong the stop.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / Rodriguez mission / officer safety\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Rodriguez circuit query → Ninth Circuit opinion → later Steinman citations\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Nazario v. Gutierrez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-05-31\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1620\",\"proposition\":\"Addressed when officers may draw and point firearms during a high-risk traffic stop and treated the force inquiry as sensitive to the driver’s delayed yielding, visibility of his hands, commands, and the changing threat over the encounter.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / firearms display / excessive force\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 published traffic-stop force query → Fourth Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chiaverini v. City of Napoleon\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2024-06-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"602 U.S. 556; No. 23-50\",\"proposition\":\"Probable cause for one charge does not categorically defeat a Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution claim concerning another charge lacking probable cause when the unsupported charge caused or prolonged pretrial detention; the Court rejected the Sixth Circuit’s any-charge rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"arrest and pretrial detention / charge-specific probable cause\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2024 Fourth Amendment docket → official opinion → circuit-rule comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Cuevas v. City of Tulare\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-10\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-15953\",\"proposition\":\"An unintended passenger struck by police gunfire could invoke the Fourth Amendment because officers had already intentionally seized the vehicle and its occupants through blocking and commands; the court separately granted qualified immunity on force reasonableness.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"show-of-authority seizure / vehicle occupants / unintended gunfire\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Torres unintended-target query → Ninth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Irish v. McNamara\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"108 F.4th 715; No. 23-3034\",\"proposition\":\"Granted qualified immunity where a police dog released to catch a suspect instead bit another officer, holding it was not clearly established whether an unintended target is seized or whether subjective target-specific intent matters. The issue later became part of an express circuit conflict.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / unintended K-9 target / qualified immunity\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Torres K-9 query → Eighth Circuit opinion → 2026 Kilnapp split discussion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Baker\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"108 F.4th 1241\",\"proposition\":\"A contemporaneous dog sniff did not violate Rodriguez; directing occupants out and requiring vehicle windows closed could be treated as negligibly burdensome safety measures while the traffic mission continued, despite the officer’s drug-investigation purpose.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / dog sniff / Rodriguez mission and duration\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Rodriguez dog-sniff query → Tenth Circuit published opinion → later Ramdial citations\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Grandia Gonzalez v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 1304\",\"proposition\":\"Held that the Fourth Amendment does not require a misdemeanor to occur in an officer’s presence before a warrantless arrest, rejecting incorporation of the common-law in-the-presence rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"warrantless misdemeanor arrest / in-the-presence rule\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS Gonzalez certiorari-denial statement → cited Eleventh Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Soukaneh v. Andrzejewski\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 21-2047\",\"proposition\":\"Lawful firearm possession and presentation of a valid permit did not justify handcuffing a motorist in a police car for more than thirty minutes or searching his vehicle; the restraint could constitute a de facto arrest without probable cause, and qualified immunity was denied.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry stop versus arrest / handcuffing / lawful firearm possession\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 firearm-related Terry query → Second Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Sanderlin v. Dwyer\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-09-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"116 F.4th 905; No. 23-15487\",\"proposition\":\"An officer’s intentional firing of a foam baton round directly at a protester could constitute a physical-force seizure where the impact temporarily restricted his movement, even though the restraint was brief.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / less-lethal projectile / protest policing\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Torres protest-force query → Ninth Circuit published opinion → comparison with Puente\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Keller\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-12-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-40338\",\"proposition\":\"At a permanent immigration checkpoint, officers may briefly stop and question motorists without individualized suspicion about citizenship and immigration status, but a vehicle search still requires consent, probable cause, or another recognized exception.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"checkpoints / permanent immigration checkpoint\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 federal checkpoint query → Fifth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Puente v. City of Phoenix\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-12-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-15344\",\"proposition\":\"Airborne chemical agents, tear gas, and flash-sound devices used to disperse a crowd ordinarily do not effect Fourth Amendment seizures when objectively intended to make people leave rather than restrain them; resulting force claims fall under substantive due process absent another seizure.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / dispersal force / objective intent\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Torres crowd-control query → Ninth Circuit opinion → contrast with Sanderlin and Cheairs\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. In\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-12-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-2917\",\"proposition\":\"Temporary handcuffing during an investigative stop did not automatically convert the detention into an arrest where specific weapon and safety concerns justified the restraint’s manner and duration.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry stop versus arrest / handcuffing\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 published handcuffing-and-arrest query → Ninth Circuit opinion\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Gonzalez v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-02-24\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-5577\",\"proposition\":\"The Court denied certiorari, but Justice Sotomayor, joined by Justice Gorsuch, identified a serious unresolved question whether founding-era common law constitutionally requires warrantless misdemeanor arrests to satisfy an in-the-presence rule and questioned whether contrary circuit precedent remains sound.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"warrantless misdemeanor arrest / historical minimum / frontier signal\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2025 Fourth Amendment orders → official statement respecting denial → Eleventh Circuit decision\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Steinman\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"130 F.4th 693; No. 23-1703\",\"proposition\":\"Unrelated questioning conducted while an officer simultaneously and diligently completed a citation did not measurably prolong the stop; ordering the driver out and obtaining a criminal-history check were permissible safety measures, and later-developed reasonable suspicion justified any ensuing extension.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / simultaneous questioning / criminal-history check\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Rodriguez query → Ninth Circuit opinion → Ramirez and Landeros comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Barnes v. Felix\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-05-15\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"605 U.S. 73; No. 23-1239\",\"proposition\":\"Rejected the Fifth Circuit’s moment-of-threat rule. Fourth Amendment force reasonableness requires all relevant circumstances, including events leading to the instant force was used; courts may not impose chronological blinders. The Court left open how officer-created danger affects reasonableness.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"excessive force / totality of circumstances / moment-of-threat rule\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2025 Fourth Amendment docket → official opinion → Fifth Circuit overruling analysis\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Puckett\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"139 F.4th 730\",\"proposition\":\"Approximately twenty seconds of unrelated questioning did not unlawfully prolong the seizure where the traffic tasks still reasonably required more time; the decision applies Rodriguez through measurable prolongation rather than a formally permissible de minimis detour.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / brief unrelated questioning / measurable prolongation\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Rodriguez circuit query → Eighth Circuit opinion → Third Circuit Ross discussion\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Wilson\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-07-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-30777\",\"proposition\":\"Police cannot conduct a Terry stop solely because a person appears to possess a firearm where carry may be lawful; the actual stop was upheld because additional particularized facts connected Wilson to a fugitive and criminal activity.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry reasonable suspicion / lawful firearm possession / post-Bruen\",\"discovery_path\":\"Post-Bruen firearm-stop query → Fifth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Cheairs v. City of Seattle\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-08-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"145 F.4th 1233; No. 24-3163\",\"proposition\":\"A blast ball intentionally directed toward the front of a crowd and striking a person could be found to manifest objective intent to restrain and therefore effect a Torres seizure; the force was nevertheless reasonable given the immediate threat attributed to the nearby crowd.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / crowd-control projectile / objective intent\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Torres protest-force query → Ninth Circuit opinion → Puente and Sanderlin comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Larremore\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-08-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-50431\",\"proposition\":\"A consensual roadside encounter became an investigative detention when the deputy objectively communicated a smuggling accusation and desire to search; incidental touching of the truck was not a Torres physical-force seizure, and ambiguous words such as “hang on a sec” must be evaluated contextually under the show-of-authority test.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"consensual encounter / show of authority / physical contact with vehicle\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Hodari and show-of-authority query → Fifth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ross\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-08-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"151 F.4th 487; No. 23-1631\",\"proposition\":\"Organized roadside questioning into four Rodriguez categories—small talk, traffic-infraction inquiries, safety inquiries, and criminal investigation. Small talk does not itself create a detour; safety-related questions require an articulable contextual connection and may not become a prolonged criminal investigation.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / questioning taxonomy / Rodriguez mission\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 precedential Rodriguez query → Third Circuit opinion → cross-circuit comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Noem v. Vasquez Perdomo\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2025-09-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25A169\",\"proposition\":\"The Court stayed an injunction limiting immigration stops. Justice Kavanaugh’s concurrence stated that apparent ethnicity alone cannot create reasonable suspicion but may be considered with location, occupation, language or accent, and behavior. The emergency order is not a final merits holding but is a major current training frontier.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry reasonable suspicion / immigration enforcement / ethnicity factor\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 immigration-stop litigation query → SCOTUS emergency docket and concurrence → commentary follow-up\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Ramdial\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-09-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-6213\",\"proposition\":\"An officer did not prolong a traffic stop by conducting unrelated internet and license-plate-reader research while awaiting dispatch’s response to legitimate license, warrant, and criminal-history inquiries; the parallel investigation added no time before reasonable suspicion arose.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / parallel investigation / Rodriguez duration\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Rodriguez database-query search → Tenth Circuit published opinion → Frazier distinction\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Watkins v. Davis\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-09-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"156 F.4th 1084; No. 23-13616\",\"proposition\":\"Officers effected a physical-force seizure by intentionally shooting an occupied vehicle to stop its driver even though bullets touched the vehicle rather than her body. The driver also could be found to have submitted to a show of authority when she first realized the shooters were police and promptly stopped.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / indirect contact / delayed recognition and submission\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Torres vehicle-shooting query → Eleventh Circuit opinion → acknowledged contrary-circuit cases\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hawatmeh v. City of Henderson\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-11-07\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"159 F.4th 591; No. 24-6146\",\"proposition\":\"A hostage accidentally struck by police gunfire aimed at his captor was not seized because the officers’ objectively manifested purpose was to rescue, not restrain, the hostage; the brief continuation of fire after the captor was hit did not alter the causal encounter.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / hostage or unintended target / objective intent\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Torres unintended-target query → Ninth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hawkins\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-12-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4502\",\"proposition\":\"Prior drug history, generalized location evidence, an ambiguous parking-lot interaction, and weakly inconsistent answers did not collectively supply reasonable suspicion to prolong a traffic stop for a drug investigation; mundane conduct cannot be repackaged into a web of suspicion.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / Rodriguez extension / reasonable suspicion limits\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Fourth Circuit Rodriguez query → published opinion → 2026 Williams citation trail\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Belton v. Loveridge\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"129 F.4th 271\",\"proposition\":\"An officer who intentionally shot a particular person seized that person even though she mistakenly believed he was the suspect; mistaken identity bears on reasonableness, not on whether intentionally applied force created a seizure.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / mistaken identity\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Kilnapp split analysis → cited Fourth Circuit precedent\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hight v. Williams\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"164 F.4th 672\",\"proposition\":\"No Fourth Amendment seizure occurred when an officer intentionally fired at a dog and a stray bullet hit the dog’s owner; the officer had not objectively applied force for the purpose of restraining the owner. This target-specific approach conflicts with Kilnapp’s broader rule.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / accidental bystander / circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Torres unintended-target query → Eighth Circuit opinion → Kilnapp conflict analysis\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Kilnapp v. City of Cleveland\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-02-18\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-3149\",\"proposition\":\"When an officer intentionally fires under circumstances objectively manifesting an intent to restrain, every person struck by that force is seized even if not the officer’s intended target. The Sixth Circuit expressly rejected subjective target-specific intent, acknowledged a circuit split, and still granted qualified immunity because the rule was not previously clear.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / unintended target / objective-intent circuit split\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Torres bystander query → published Sixth Circuit opinion → sister-circuit survey\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Williams\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1510\",\"proposition\":\"A Michigan v. Long protective search was unreasonable after the driver had been arrested and secured where the passenger-owner was calm, cooperative, lawfully present, suspected of no crime, made no furtive movement, and presented no articulable danger or access-to-weapons risk.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry frisk / vehicle protective search / Michigan v. Long\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Terry vehicle-frisk query → Tenth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hollamon v. County of Wright\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-2795\",\"proposition\":\"Pepperballs used to repel protesters breaching secured property did not effect a Fourth Amendment seizure where the force objectively sought dispersal or exclusion and did not terminate movement; the claim therefore could not proceed as Fourth Amendment excessive force.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / pepperballs / dispersal versus restraint\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 protest-force query → Eighth Circuit published opinion → Puente comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Zorn v. Linton\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-03-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. 568; No. 25-297\",\"proposition\":\"An officer received qualified immunity for using a routine rear wristlock to lift a passively resistant sit-in protester after repeated warnings; prior circuit precedent did not clearly establish with sufficient factual specificity that this degree of force was unconstitutional. The Court did not definitively resolve the merits.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"arrest force / passive resistance / qualified immunity\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2026 Fourth Amendment orders → per curiam opinion → Second Circuit precedent comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Martin\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-04-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-4233\",\"proposition\":\"An officer impermissibly extended a parking-related stop from its inception by immediately abandoning its resolved traffic purpose for firearms and contraband questioning. Firearms questions are not automatically mission-related safety inquiries absent objective circumstances indicating danger.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"traffic stop / firearms questioning / Rodriguez mission\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Rodriguez firearms-question query → Fourth Circuit published opinion → cross-circuit disagreement in dissent\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"District of Columbia v. R.W.\",\"court\":\"U.S. Supreme Court\",\"date\":\"2026-04-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"608 U.S. 22; No. 25-248\",\"proposition\":\"Reasonable suspicion existed based on the full combination of a late-night suspicious-vehicle report, the companions’ unprovoked flight, and the driver backing away with the door open. Courts may not excise or isolate facts, and companions’ flight can inform suspicion about a remaining driver in a common context.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Terry reasonable suspicion / totality / associates’ flight\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2026 Fourth Amendment docket → official per curiam opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Martinez v. Hinojosa\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-06-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-40535\",\"proposition\":\"A person whom officers deliberately shot was seized even though they mistakenly believed he was someone else; target misidentification does not negate objective intent to restrain the person actually shot and instead belongs in the reasonableness analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"physical-force seizure / mistaken identity / Torres\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Torres mistaken-target query → Fifth Circuit published opinion → Belton comparison\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Williams\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-07-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-4512\",\"proposition\":\"Patrol cars positioned to partially box in a parked vehicle effected a show-of-authority seizure even though a driver might escape through complicated maneuvering. A vague anonymous drug tip plus presence in a high-crime area did not provide reasonable suspicion for that seizure.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"show-of-authority seizure / police positioning / anonymous tip\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Hodari vehicle-blocking query → Fourth Circuit published opinion\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
